Root cause of the "Failed to set beacon parameter for interface: wifi0
error: -95(Operation not supported)" failure that blocked the controller's
VAPs from ever beaconing under mac80211_hwsim -- and it's a genuine bug, not
an hwsim-only quirk.

Root-caused via a raw netlink capture (strace -x on the exact
NL80211_CMD_START_AP sendmsg for wifi0, decoded with pyroute2's own nl80211
attribute parser to remove any doubt about manual byte-counting errors) after
ruling out every content-based hypothesis (802.11ax/be, MLO, spectrum
management, WPA3/SAE all confirmed absent from the message, yet -EOPNOTSUPP
persisted). Decisive control test: plain vanilla hostapd against the exact
same wiphy/interface (wlan0) with a minimal config succeeded cleanly
(AP-ENABLED) -- proving mac80211_hwsim can start an AP fine, and the fault is
specific to something wifi_hal's own message includes that hostapd's own
driver_nl80211.c doesn't.

That difference: NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS and NL80211_ATTR_ACL_POLICY, present
even though this VAP has ACL disabled (mac_filter_enable == false).
nl80211_put_acl()'s disabled branch does:

    nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_ACL_POLICY, NL80211_ACL_POLICY_ACCEPT_UNLESS_LISTED);
    nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS, 0);

NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS's kernel netlink attribute policy expects a NESTED
attribute (a list of MAC addresses, built via nla_nest_start/nla_nest_end --
see the *enabled* branch just above, which does this correctly), not a plain
u32. Sending it as a bare u32 violates that policy, and cfg80211 rejects the
entire START_AP/NEW_BEACON request with -EOPNOTSUPP wherever the driver's
attribute policy is validated strictly. This isn't an hwsim-specific gap --
it's a wrong attribute type -- so the fix applies unconditionally to all
platforms, not gated behind HWSIM_RADIO like the earlier default-config
patches in this series.

Two call sites have the same bug: nl80211_put_acl() (called directly inside
wifi_drv_set_ap(), i.e. this is the exact function/message this bug was
found in) and the standalone nl80211_set_acl() (NL80211_CMD_SET_MAC_ACL,
used for runtime ACL updates -- not yet observed to be exercised in this
container, but fixed for consistency since it's the identical bug).

Fix: nl80211_put_acl() just omits both attributes when ACL is disabled --
their absence already means "no restriction" to the kernel, so nothing needs
to be sent at all. nl80211_set_acl() keeps sending ACL_POLICY (since that
command's whole purpose is ACL state) but uses a properly nested, empty MAC
list instead of the malformed u32 -- ACCEPT_UNLESS_LISTED with zero entries
is functionally identical to "disabled" (every station accepted).

---
 src/wifi_hal_nl80211.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
@@ -16329,8 +16329,14 @@
                                                                                  "Whitelist");

     } else {
-        nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_ACL_POLICY, NL80211_ACL_POLICY_ACCEPT_UNLESS_LISTED);
-        nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS, 0);
+        /* NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS is a nested attribute (a list of MAC addresses);
+         * sending it as a bare nla_put_u32(..., 0) violates its kernel netlink
+         * attribute policy (expects NLA_NESTED, not NLA_U32), so cfg80211 rejects
+         * the whole START_AP/NEW_BEACON request with -EOPNOTSUPP wherever the
+         * driver's policy is validated strictly (confirmed via a raw netlink
+         * capture against mac80211_hwsim). Simply omitting both attributes when
+         * ACL is disabled is correct and sufficient: their absence already means
+         * "no ACL restriction" to the kernel. */
         wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: Disable ACL\n", __func__, __LINE__);
     }

@@ -16404,16 +16410,26 @@
             vap->u.bss_info.mac_filter_mode == wifi_mac_filter_mode_black_list ? "Blacklist" : "Whitelist");

     } else {
+        /* NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS is a nested attribute (a list of MAC addresses);
+         * nla_put_u32(..., 0) sends it as a plain u32 instead, violating its
+         * kernel netlink attribute policy (see nl80211_put_acl() above for the
+         * same bug, confirmed via a raw netlink capture against
+         * mac80211_hwsim). Use a properly nested, empty list instead: with
+         * ACCEPT_UNLESS_LISTED and zero entries this is functionally identical
+         * to "disabled" (every station is accepted) but doesn't violate the
+         * attribute's expected type. */
         if (nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_ACL_POLICY, NL80211_ACL_POLICY_ACCEPT_UNLESS_LISTED) < 0 ){
             wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: nl80211: Failed to set ACL policy\n", __func__, __LINE__);
             nlmsg_free(msg);
             return -ENOMEM;
         }
-        if (nla_put_u32(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS, 0) < 0) {
-            wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: nl80211: Failed to accept ACL Policy\n", __func__, __LINE__);
+        acl = nla_nest_start(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MAC_ADDRS);
+        if (acl == NULL) {
+            wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: nl80211: Failed to add empty ACL list to msg\n", __func__, __LINE__);
             nlmsg_free(msg);
             return -ENOMEM;
         }
+        nla_nest_end(msg, acl);
         wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: Disable ACL\n", __func__, __LINE__);
     }
